= OODA loop — Technical =

== Scope and historical source ==
The '''observe–orient–decide–act (OODA) loop''' is a strategic account of adaptive interaction associated with John R. Boyd. Its most recognizable diagram appears in ''The Essence of Winning and Losing'' dated 28 June 1995. Boyd's work was developed through briefings rather than a single conventional peer-reviewed model paper. The 2018 Air University Press compilation reproduces the diagram and provides editorial historical context.<ref name="BoydEdition">John R. Boyd, ''A Discourse on Winning and Losing'', ed. Grant T. Hammond, Air University Press, 2018, introduction and appendix pp. 383–386, reproducing ''The Essence of Winning and Losing'' (28 June 1995). [https://media.defense.gov/2018/May/22/2001920668/-1/-1/0/B_0151_Boyd_Discourse_Winning_Losing.pdf Official PDF].</ref>

== Structural reading of the 1995 diagram ==
The diagram is more complex than a four-node ring. It depicts:
* '''Observations''' receiving unfolding circumstances, environmental interaction and outside information.
* '''Orientation''' integrating genetic heritage, cultural traditions, previous experience, new information, and analysis/synthesis. These are Boyd's conceptual labels, not empirically isolated neurological modules.
* '''Decision''' explicitly labeled a ''hypothesis'' and '''Action''' a ''test''.
* Multiple feedback and feed-forward paths, including '''implicit guidance and control''' that can shape observation and action without a discrete explicit decision.

Orientation is therefore not merely a middle stage; it influences what counts as relevant evidence, what options are perceived and how action is selected. The diagram describes an adaptive interpretive process, not a deterministic four-step finite-state machine.<ref name="BoydEdition"/>

== Competitive and organizational contexts ==
In military applications, an opponent may be affected by the tempo, unpredictability and coherence of another actor's behavior. US Marine Corps ''MCDP 1-3: Tactics'' describes time-competitive observation–orientation–decision–action cycles and emphasizes the need to adjust to changed circumstances.<ref name="MarineTactics">United States Marine Corps, ''MCDP 1-3: Tactics'', discussion of Boyd's theory of competitive decision cycles. [https://www.marines.mil/portals/1/publications/mcdp%201-3%20tactics.pdf Official PDF].</ref> This is evidence of doctrinal adoption, not an independent controlled trial establishing a universal performance advantage.

The framework can also be used heuristically in organizational incident management: observe new signals, test interpretations, choose an intervention, and evaluate its effects. This is an illustrative transfer of concepts. It should not be presented as an experimentally validated causal theory of business performance or as an AI-agent architecture without a specified implementation and evaluation.

== Distinctions and counterexamples ==
'''Speed versus adaptive quality.''' A short cycle can be harmful when observations are noisy, orientation is biased, actions are irreversible or feedback is delayed. In a medical or safety-critical decision, appropriate verification may be more important than tempo. No single cycle-duration threshold is established by Boyd's diagram.

'''Loop versus rigid pipeline.''' A workflow that forces each activity through exactly four serialized stages misses the implicit guidance paths and continuous reinterpretation in the 1995 source. Conversely, merely labeling any iterative workflow OODA does not establish that it instantiates Boyd's strategic ideas.

'''Model versus measurement.''' OODA is a conceptual and doctrinal framework. A testable operationalization would need to define observable events, decision quality, latency, adaptation, context and outcomes, and compare a specified intervention with credible alternatives. Neither the four labels nor the original diagram supplies such a measurement instrument.

== Worked qualitative analysis ==
Consider an incident team observing an elevated error rate after a release. One hypothesis attributes the failures to the release; an alternative attributes them to an upstream dependency. A reversible rollback tests the first explanation, while dependency telemetry can discriminate between them. Feedback changes orientation: if the error rate persists after rollback, the initial hypothesis loses support. This example illustrates the interplay between interpretation, action and feedback, but it does not establish the framework's effectiveness or prescribe a universal response sequence.

== Provenance and limitations ==
Boyd's 1995 sketch is the primary conceptual authority. The 2018 official edition and Marine Corps doctrine establish provenance and use. They do not provide randomized evidence that following the slogan improves performance. Cultural and historical language in the diagram should be interpreted as Boyd's framing, not as established causal partitions of cognition. Quantitative equations, universal success rates and claims of inevitable superiority are intentionally omitted.

